      "widgets_values": [
        "# Pulse Slate 3 · one window (under 15 s)\n\n**The short path, and the one verified on hardware.** A timeline that fits in a\nsingle H3 window needs no Pulse Render node at all: `PulseSlate` hands back\n`positive` and `latent` and your own sampler does the rest.\n\nThis is the only graph where those two outputs are live. Past one window they are\ndeliberately **blocked** — they would be the last window alone, which is not the\nfilm — and the timeline goes to Pulse Render instead. See `PulseSlate_LongForm.json`.\n\n## What to read first\n\nQueue it and read **③ compiled prompt**. That is the literal text going to the\nmodel: six sections in MiniMax's reference-mode order, with every `@Name`\nalready resolved to the tag the tokenizer will assign. A wrong reference binding\nrenders successfully and gives you the wrong film, so read it before you spend\nGPU time, not after.\n\n## The chain\n\n`ModelSamplingMiniMaxH3` (sigma shift) sits between the checkpoint and the\nsampler. It is not optional — it is what sets the flow schedule H3 was trained\non. `PulseSlate` carries its own `shift_video` / `shift_audio` widgets for the\nreport and for the long path; on this path the node in the graph is what counts,\nand the two are set to the same values here.\n\n`cfg` stays at 1.0, which selects `BasicGuider`. H3's reference pipeline has no\nnegative conditioning anywhere.\n\n## Where the patches go\n\nAttention and offload patches belong between `UNETLoader` and\n`ModelSamplingMiniMaxH3`:\n\n```\nUNETLoader\n  → SageAttention / Sol-Attn        (attention)\n  → SpectrumApplyMiniMaxH3          (history_storage: system_ram)\n  → ModelSamplingMiniMaxH3\n  → BasicGuider / BasicScheduler\n```\n\nNothing third-party ships wired in, so this file loads clean without them. On a\n32 GB card `system_ram` is what makes a full-length window fit at all.\n"
      ],
      "title": "READ ME"
    }
